speechtext:
To my mind, the facts
stated by some of my hon. friends are the best arguments I have yet heard in favour of the famous Harry J. Flynn gratuity scheme. That is to say, lend a certain amount of money to a returned soldier and shortly afterwards he will be able to pay you back. The Government, having done that, congratulates itself on being the means of enabling certain soldiers to speculate on land and return the money which they had borrowed.
From what I have heard here this afternoon apparently a large number of soldiers have settled on land that had previously been occupied, I understand from my hon. friend (Mr. Caldwell) that in New Brunswick at any rate the farms purchased were as a rule farms that had already been occupied. Now if this scheme of land settlement is to be of any permanent use to the country, in my opinion we should make it a colonization scheme, not a scheme for placing soldiers on the land in order that other people occupying that land may come to the cities. That is how it would appear to have worked out in New Brunswick at any rate, and from the little knowledge I have of the scheme in Quebec I would think the same condition exists there. If this is not a colonization scheme, but simply a scheme for the advantage of certain returned soldiers, I say that all returned soldiers should be entitled to equal benefits under it-another argument in favour of the gratuity. In passing I might say that I have never been in favour of the gratuity, and I am not now, but the very reasons which the Government gives for congratulating itself might be used by those who do not favour its policy in urging their own claim.
I should also like to call the attention of the Government to some criticisms by a certain body favourably and generally known as the Great War Veterans' Association. This body is a very large and representative one, and its members are the people who are supposed to be benefited under this scheme. It is all very well for

members of the Blouse representing the agricultural classes to state that the scheme as a whole has worked well, and I am glad that the minister has been fortunate enough to receive their congratulations, as I am sure he is glad, but if this scheme does not meet with the approval of those presumed to be benefited under it, I think it * must lack something, and it is the duty of the minister and of the Government to see whether any remedy suggested cannot be applied. I had here a few moments ago, until my desk mate disappeared, a number of resolutions passed by the Great War Veterans' Association. However, I have in my hand a newspaper report quoting certain of these resolutions, as to which I think the minister should make some statement. The text of the resolution adopted by the Great War Veterans' Association in convention assembled reads in part as follows:
And whereas the present Soldiers' Settlement Act gives wide latitude in the promulgation of regulations to the Soldiers' Settlement Board;
And whereas said regulations have been, in many instances, unnecessaily rigorous and designed for idealistic conditions which do not exist;
And whereas, in many instances, hardship has ensued to our comrades by reason of the administration and general inefficiency of the Soldiers' Settlement Board;
And whereas said regulations, in many instances, break the pledges made to the soldier, while in the trenches, in regard to land settlement ;
Therefore be it resolved that the Great War Veterans' Association of Canada memorialize Parliament to entirely reorganize, after investigation, the present administration of the Soldiers' Settlement Board.
Before proceeding to the other resolutions I would ask the minister to tell the committee whether he has taken any steps to meet the wishes of the organized veterans with respect to reorganizing the Soldiers' Settlement Board, and if not, why.
